ne_margin": 15.29, "analyst_adjustment": -0.79, "key_factors": ["Davids has raised $4.5M to Jenkins' $47K, an overwhelming financial edge", "All ten expert ratings place the race at Safe or Solid Democratic", "Incumbent Davids has repeatedly won a district that leans only modestly Democratic", "Prediction markets price Davids at roughly 95% to win"], "rationale": "Every available signal points the same way: unanimous Safe or Solid Democratic ratings, a roughly 95-to-1 fundraising advantage for Davids, her proven incumbency in a D+2 district, and market pricing near 95%. With no public polling, the forecast rests on these fundamentals, which favor the incumbent by a wide margin.", "watch": "A late surge of outside Republican spending or a sharply improved national environment for the GOP could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Sharice Davids (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Sharice Davids seeks another term in the Kansas City-area 3rd District, which leans slightly Democratic. She won her primary easily, while Republican Eric Jenkins enters the general election with little funding or visibility. No independent public polling tracks the race, so the picture rests on fundamentals and expert assessments.\n\nEvery major race rating puts the contest in Safe or Solid Democratic territory, and prediction markets give Davids roughly a 95 percent chance of winning. She has raised about $4.5 million to Jenkins' $47,000, an advantage that supports sustained advertising while he remains largely off the air. A Libertarian is also on the ballot. The district's modest lean means a strong Republican national environment could tighten the race, but Davids' incumbency, fundraising, and record of bipartisan legislation make her a clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9826, "margin": 15.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0